// TEST: Static Bound Forward Ref Violated
// ============================================================================
// The NEGATIVE half of structs/050. A bound naming a `let static` declared
// below it must not merely RESOLVE — it must still be ENFORCED, with the
// resolved value.
//
// This is the failure mode a partial fix leaves behind. Once the static
// dependency edge exists, structs/050 passes; a fix that made `zz_lim` visible
// but lost the resulting conjunct would ALSO make it pass, and nothing else in
// the corpus would notice. So the check is pinned on a value that is legal
// under a wrong reading and illegal under the right one: `m = 3` is outside
// every interpretation of [-2, 2], and the only way to accept it is to have
// stopped checking.
//
// NAMING IS LOAD-BEARING HERE TOO — see structs/050's header for the full
// argument. Static resolution drains a Map in KEY order, so `a_bad` is named
// to sort BEFORE `zz_lim` under any comparer: without the dependency edge it
// folds first and dies on an undefined variable rather than on the bound. Do
// not rename either binding to something descriptive.
//
// The conjunct index is load-bearing as well. `Plain` declares no
// where-conjuncts, so its list is the desugared bounds in field-declaration
// order, Lo before Hi: conjunct 1 is `-zz_lim <= m`, conjunct 2 is
// `m <= zz_lim`. Naming conjunct 2 pins that the upper bound is what failed,
// that `max=` is inclusive (3 is the first violation above 2), and that both
// endpoints survived resolution — a bound that had collapsed to one conjunct
// would renumber this.
// ============================================================================
struct Plain {
    m: Int<min=-zz_lim, max=zz_lim>
}

let static a_bad = Plain { m = 3 }

let static zz_lim = 2
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: Constraint violation in Plain (static, conjunct 2)
